Application model
- Keep application-specific behavior in the application workspace. Change
  Wharfie only for a verified framework gap within the requested scope.
- Preserve the developer's CLI arguments, help, stdio, and exit semantics.
  Packaged Wharfie operator commands live under <app> wharfie.
- Default-export defineApp(...) from wharfie.app.js. Start with the supported
  shorthand for an ordinary CLI:

    import { defineApp } from '@wharfie/wharfie/app';
    export default defineApp({ id: 'my-app', main: './src/cli.js' });

- Verify the entrypoint export contract against the current minimal example.
  Add named activities and finite workflows only where the task needs durable
  execution. The explicit schemaVersion: 4 form remains available; do not
  expose internal ActorSystem/resource abstractions as an authoring API.
- When ordinary arguments should select a default workflow, use the documented
  cli.durable adapter. It validates/projects argv to bounded JSON and performs
  no business work or external mutations itself.
- Add workflow-only UTC schedules for actual recurrence. Use the supported
  cron grammar and bounded workflow inputs from the current contract.
- Put service clients and domain logic in ordinary modules. Use environment
  configuration for credentials, never manifest resources or embedded secrets.
- Direct external calls are unmanaged effects. Use runtime.effects only when
  its documented guarantees fit; do not silently retry interrupted unsafe work.
- Exact activity externalPackages must agree with the application's npm lock
  and supported frozen dependency closure. Do not rely on an ambient install.

Implementation and validation
1. Define the useful application outcome and its observable acceptance test.
2. Run relevant existing application checks and note pre-existing failures.
3. Implement the CLI/domain logic, then the smallest needed Wharfie manifest.
4. Test domain behavior and CLI semantics using local fixtures and mocked
   external services. Keep ordinary tests independent of live credentials.
5. Validate the manifest and the execution paths the app uses. From the
   Wharfie checkout, the local launcher supports:
     node ./bin/wharfie app manifest <application-directory>
     node ./bin/wharfie app run <activity> --dir <application-directory>
6. Package only when the requested outcome needs distribution. Verify the
   selected target and run the artifact in an independent environment.
7. If cloud self-deployment is required, follow the current packaged operator
   guide. A deployment-capable artifact must carry its verified Linux payload;
   AWS additionally needs the exact matching companion at build time. Sharing
